Short answer: LLMs can reason about patterns, but they can’t reliably change the browser’s focus model. You need generic driver primitives to let the LLM direct focus, then type.
What I did now:
Added a generic action the LLM can call: type=focus_contenteditable with optional selector. It focuses the nearest visible contenteditable (no Notion-specific logic).
Kept observe exposing activeElement details so the LLM can detect traps and then call focus_contenteditable.

No app names, no specific attributes required in prompts.
LLM decides when to call focus_contenteditable; the driver just executes a generic operation.


